fuse: block DAX faults while dropping entries on a size change

Since fs DAX pages became refcounted folios, removing the entry of a page
that a process still maps or pins corrupts the folio: the warnings in
dax_folio_put(), dax_insert_entry() and zap_huge_pmd() fire, the process's
rss counters go wrong, and a later wait for the page to go idle never ends.

The O_TRUNC open and the truncating setattr already take invalidate_lock
and break the layouts before they truncate. Four other paths did not:

- an atomic create that truncates an existing file,
- a setattr whose reply shows the size changed, which also invalidated
  the whole mapping afterwards,
- an attribute refresh that sees the server changed the size,
- copy_file_range, which dropped the destination's entries without even
  unmapping them first.

truncate_pagecache() unmaps before it deletes entries, but a fault can map
a page again in between. Give the first three a DAX variant that blocks
faults and waits for pinned pages from the PMD holding the new size on,
and skip the setattr invalidation for DAX, which has no page cache.
copy_file_range has nothing to drop for DAX: the destination's pages are
the host's, so they already hold the copied data.
---
--- a/fs/fuse/dax.c
+++ b/fs/fuse/dax.c
@@ -691,6 +691,24 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * truncate_pagecache() for a DAX inode. Fs DAX pages are refcounted folios,
+ * so removing the entry of a page that a process still maps or pins corrupts
+ * the folio. Block faults so nothing maps a page again between the unmap and
+ * the removal of its entry, and wait for pinned pages first. Start at the PMD
+ * holding the new size: a huge entry there also covers pages below it.
+ */
+void fuse_dax_truncate_pagecache(struct inode *inode, loff_t newsize)
+{
+	struct address_space *mapping = inode->i_mapping;
+
+	filemap_invalidate_lock(mapping);
+	/* On a signal, faults stay blocked; only a pin can still race. */
+	fuse_dax_break_layouts(inode, round_down(newsize, PMD_SIZE), -1);
+	truncate_pagecache(inode, newsize);
+	filemap_invalidate_unlock(mapping);
+}
